2|0@Design^1438890158#ctty5cq:TTUporter$:Not OP, but his comment is far from a cheap shot. He called the appearance of the logo's intended figure into question and rightly so. Logos are communicators, and they are only as effective as the image , feeling, and meaning they communicate to the viewer. Logos inherently have to deal with the intentional fallacy, because it doesn't matter the intention of the designer if the intended message fails to get conveyed to the intended audience. Design is the vehicle for this communication. The logo gives off the appearance of a surprised lion due to a combination of eye shape and head tilt. If this is in line with the brand identity of Penn State, then by all means the logo is successful, however if the university wants to give the perception that it is a strong, proud university, then perhaps they would be wise to listen to this critique.
